  "spoken_transcript": "Under three months, a fever of 38°C or more needs immediate medical care. At three to six months, 39°C or more needs prompt medical assessment. If your child is sleeping comfortably, there's no need to wake them for antipyretics. Antipyretics reduce fever and discomfort, helping your child rest and recover. Repeated fever over 40°C, or fever lasting three days, needs medical review. For older active children, monitor closely at home and seek care if they worsen. Look for rashes, ear or neck pain, and changes in urine output. Use a fever reducer only if your child is uncomfortable, following its age, weight, and dosing instructions. For febrile seizure, decreased consciousness, no urination for 8+ hours, or rash with joint pain, go to the ER. Keep monitoring your child; if fever persists or worsens, consult your pediatrician.",
